	"why do I need to learn SQL, I can 
	ask AI/LLM to generate it for me"?

Great question — and you'll definitely get it. 
Here's how I'd frame the answer:

You wouldn't trust a financial report you can't 
read. Same principle applies here. AI can generate 
SQL, but if you don't understand what it wrote, you 
can't tell whether it gave you the right answer or 
a plausible-sounding wrong one. And in business, 
acting on a wrong query — the wrong revenue number, 
the wrong customer count, the wrong inventory total 
— has real consequences.

Think of SQL like reading a balance sheet. 
Could you ask someone else to read it for you? 
Sure. But no hiring manager wants a finance 
professional who can't read a balance sheet 
themselves. 

The same is true for data. Employers expect 
business professionals to be able to ask questions 
of data directly, verify the answers, and have an 
informed conversation with engineers and analysts. 
"I just ask AI" is not a skill you put on a resume.

There's also a practical reality: AI works best 
when you can guide it. If you know SQL, you can 
write a precise prompt, catch mistakes in the output, 
and fix edge cases. If you don't know SQL, you're 
guessing whether the AI got it right — and you'll 
never know when it didn't.

The goal of this course isn't to compete with AI. 
It's to make you the person who knows enough to 
use AI effectively and catch it when it's wrong.


You don’t need to memorize every SQL command anymore. 
But you do need enough SQL to know whether the AI 
gave you the right answer—or merely a convincing one.

For example, suppose you ask:

> “Show me revenue by customer.”

The AI generates:

```sql
SELECT c.name, 
       SUM(o.total) AS revenue
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
JOIN order_items i ON o.id = i.order_id
GROUP BY c.name;
```

It looks reasonable, but joining `order_items` 
duplicates each order once per item. A three-item 
order worth $100 becomes $300. The query runs 
successfully and produces polished—but incorrect—results. 
Understanding joins and cardinality helps you catch that.

Or you ask:

> “Find customers who haven’t ordered this year.”

The AI uses:

```sql
WHERE customer_id NOT IN (
  SELECT customer_id FROM orders
);
```

If the subquery contains even one `NULL`, 
the query may return no customers at all. 
Knowing how `NULL` works tells you that 
`NOT EXISTS` is usually safer.

There are also questions AI cannot resolve 
from the schema alone. If you ask for 
“active customers,” does that mean customers 
who logged in recently, placed an order, have 
a paid subscription, or have not cancelled? 
AI can write valid SQL for any of those 
definitions—but only you can confirm which 
definition matches the business question.

And sometimes the risk is operational. 
An AI-generated statement such as:

```sql
UPDATE subscriptions
SET status = 'expired'
WHERE renewal_date < CURRENT_DATE;
```

might expire trials, accounts in a grace period, 
or subscriptions with pending payments. Before 
executing it, you need to inspect the condition, 
preview the affected rows with `SELECT`, understand 
transactions, and know how to roll back safely.

So the goal is not to beat AI at remembering syntax. 
Learn the durable concepts:

- tables, keys, and relationships;
- joins and row duplication;
- filtering, grouping, and aggregation;
- `NULL` behavior;
- transactions and safe updates;
- basic query performance;
- validation techniques such as row counts and spot checks.


Then use AI for what it does well: 

* recalling syntax, 
* producing boilerplate, 
* explaining unfamiliar queries, and 
* suggesting alternatives.

> AI can remove much of the typing. It cannot take responsibility for the result.

Learning SQL turns AI from a confident query generator 
into a tool you can direct, challenge, and trust appropriately.


